(23, 1, 'text', 'A1.3 Reading\nRead the text and choose the option that best matches its meaning. The correct answer may express the idea in different words.\nA Change of Plan at River Park\nOn Saturday afternoon, Ela and her cousin Kerem go to River Park with Ela\'s grandmother. Ela likes drawing, Kerem likes games and sports, and her grandmother enjoys watching birds but does not like walking long distances. The park has a cafe near the entrance, a lake, a playground and a small bird garden.\nThey leave home earlier than Kerem expected. A school sports event is taking place near the park, and Ela\'s father has told them that buses will probably fill up later. When they arrive, Kerem wants to run to the playground. However, Ela\'s grandmother feels tired after the journey, so they sit beside the cafe for a few minutes. Ela begins to draw the ducks on the lake while Kerem drinks lemonade.\nSoon dark clouds appear and the wind becomes stronger. The family had planned to walk around the lake, but they go to the covered bird garden instead because it is nearby. After a short shower, the sky clears. Kerem later plays football with two children he meets, while Ela finishes her picture.\nOn the way home, Ela\'s grandmother smiles and says, \'I\'m glad we didn\'t try to do everything at once.\'', NULL, NULL, 1, '2026-08-26 11:20:27', '2026-08-26 11:20:27'),
(24, 2, 'text', 'A2.1 Vocabulary in Context', NULL, NULL, 1, '2026-08-26 11:20:27', '2026-08-26 11:20:27'),
(25, 2, 'text', 'A2.2 Grammar in Context', NULL, NULL, 1, '2026-08-26 11:20:27', '2026-08-26 11:20:27'),
(26, 2, 'text', 'A2.3 Reading\nRead the text carefully. Some questions require you to connect information from more than one sentence.\nBorrow, Don\'t Buy?\nWhen Yusuf moved into a small flat for six months, he needed a drill to put up two shelves. Buying one for a ten-minute job seemed unnecessary, so a friend told him about a local \'Library of Things\'. Members can borrow practical items such as tools, carpet cleaners, sewing machines and camping equipment.\nYusuf joined online and reserved the drill for the following Saturday. At the centre, a volunteer showed him how to use it safely and asked him to bring it back before Monday evening. Yusuf had expected a place that simply handed out objects, but he noticed people also exchanging advice about repairs and home projects. A month later, he returned to borrow a projector for a family birthday.\nThe organisers say the service has two aims: to reduce the number of rarely used products people buy and to make useful equipment affordable for more residents. The system does have limits. Items that many people want may be booked several days ahead, and one late return can affect the next person. To reduce this problem, members receive reminder messages before an item is due back. Short demonstrations are also offered for equipment that people may not know how to use.\nYusuf says he has saved some money, but that is not the main reason he keeps using the service. He likes being able to try equipment before deciding whether he would use it often enough to own one.', NULL, NULL, 1, '2026-08-26 11:20:27', '2026-08-26 11:20:27'),
(27, 3, 'text', 'B1.1 Vocabulary in Context', NULL, NULL, 1, '2026-08-26 11:20:27', '2026-08-26 11:20:27'),
(28, 3, 'text', 'B1.2 Grammar in Context', NULL, NULL, 1, '2026-08-26 11:20:27', '2026-08-26 11:20:27'),
(29, 3, 'text', 'B1.3 Reading\nRead for detailed meaning, inference, global meaning and the writer\'s reasoning. Do not choose an option simply because it repeats a word from the text.\nThe Quiet Hour Experiment\nAt a software company called Northline, employees had begun complaining that a normal working morning felt like a series of interruptions. A message would arrive, then a short meeting, then another question from a colleague. None of these events took very long, but people said it was difficult to return to tasks that required careful thought. Managers first suggested that staff simply close their messaging apps when they needed to concentrate. This had little effect because employees worried that silence might be seen as unhelpful.\nThe company then tried a six-week experiment. Between 9:30 and 11:00 each morning, internal meetings were not allowed, and employees were told that they did not need to reply to ordinary messages during that period. Urgent matters could still be handled by phone. Some workers welcomed the idea immediately; others were doubtful. Customer-support staff, in particular, pointed out that their jobs depended on responding quickly to people outside the company.\nAfter the first week, the rules were adjusted. Teams dealing directly with customers could answer external messages as usual, while still avoiding unnecessary internal meetings. Managers also stopped judging the experiment by the number of messages sent and instead looked at whether projects moved forward and deadlines were met. By the end of six weeks, many employees reported that they were completing complicated tasks earlier in the day. However, the results were less noticeable in roles where work naturally involved frequent short conversations.\nNorthline decided to continue the quiet period but did not describe it as a perfect solution. Managers said it worked because the rules were clear about what could wait and what could not. They also admitted that no timetable can repair poor planning or unclear responsibilities. For some teams, the main benefit was not the silence itself but having permission to protect a part of the day from low-priority demands.', NULL, NULL, 1, '2026-08-26 11:20:27', '2026-08-26 11:20:27'),
(30, 4, 'text', 'B2.1 Vocabulary in Context', NULL, NULL, 1, '2026-08-26 11:20:27', '2026-08-26 11:20:27'),
(31, 4, 'text', 'B2.2 Grammar in Context', NULL, NULL, 1, '2026-08-26 11:20:27', '2026-08-26 11:20:27'),
(32, 4, 'text', 'B2.3 Reading\nRead the argument as a whole. Questions may test implication, purpose, causal reasoning and the writer\'s qualified position. Correct answers are paraphrased rather than copied from the passage.\nWhen Recommendations Start Choosing for Us\nPersonalised recommendations are now built into music platforms, shopping sites and news feeds. At first glance, they seem merely to save time by filtering an overwhelming number of choices. This convenience is real, but researchers are increasingly interested in a less obvious effect: recommendation systems do not simply predict preferences; repeated exposure can gradually help create them.\nA streaming service may learn that someone often chooses quiet acoustic music after work and therefore offer more of it. The user may welcome this. But if alternative genres appear less often, the person\'s behaviour becomes a poor guide to what they might have enjoyed under different conditions. The system can then treat its own previous recommendations as evidence of a stable taste. This feedback loop may be relatively harmless in entertainment, but it becomes more consequential when similar processes influence news, job opportunities or education.\nOne experiment with online articles changed only the order in which topics appeared. Participants who were shown a wider mixture did not report lower satisfaction, yet they ended up reading a broader range than those given highly personalised lists. The researchers did not conclude that personalisation was harmful. Instead, the result suggested that convenience and variety are not always opposites: small design choices can preserve exploration without forcing people to search from scratch.\nCompanies argue, reasonably, that users can ignore suggestions. Yet that assumes every option receives equal attention. In practice, people tend to choose what is immediately visible, especially when tired or in a hurry. The issue, therefore, is not whether algorithms remove freedom altogether; they rarely do. It is whether the architecture of choice quietly makes some paths much easier than others.\nA sensible response is not to eliminate recommendation systems. Few people want to sort through thousands of songs or products unaided. More useful would be features that allow users to adjust how adventurous recommendations are, understand why an item has appeared, and occasionally reset the history influencing future suggestions. Such controls would make personalisation less of an invisible background process and more of a tool people can actively direct.\nUltimately, convenience should not be confused with neutrality. A system that reduces effort also shapes attention. The goal should be to keep the benefits of guidance while leaving genuine room for discovery.', NULL, NULL, 1, '2026-08-26 11:20:27', '2026-08-26 11:20:27'),
